\description{
A Helper Function that compiles a STATIC (i.e. non-interactive) BaseMap of the South East
Atlantic Fisheries Organisation (SEAFO) Convention Area (CA) and its surrounding geographic
context. The map is drawn on the ACTIVE GRAPHICS DEVICE with the base R \code{graphics} package,
using the SAME spatial layers, layer toggles and area labels as the interactive
\code{\link[=maps_dynamic.compile.basemap.seafo]{maps_dynamic.compile.basemap.seafo()}} function (i.e. the SEAFO Convention Area, the
SEAFO Exclusive Economic Zones and the SEAFO VME Closures), and equips the resulting figure
with a legend, a map scale bar and a graticule. This function was created to support the
routine data-analysis work conducted by the Scientific Committee (SC) of the SEAFO - most
notably the publication-ready figures needed in reports and working documents - but may also
be used as a quick "situational awareness" BaseMap in other (3rd Party) R libraries and custom
R functions, as long as the \code{SEAFO} library is linked (i.e. previously installed and/or
specified as a library dependency).
}
\details{
\subsection{Static vs. dynamic output}{

This function is the STATIC sibling of \code{\link[=maps_dynamic.compile.basemap.seafo]{maps_dynamic.compile.basemap.seafo()}}. The
two functions share the same signature (i.e. arguments, defaults and semantics) and consume
the SAME spatial layers; only the OUTPUT differs. The dynamic version returns a \code{leaflet}
\code{htmlwidget} that is panned, zoomed, measured and toggled in a browser, whereas this static
version paints a fixed figure with base R \code{graphics}, which is what is needed for reports,
Working Documents, posters and other print media. The static figure is deliberately
dependency-free (i.e. it needs NO \code{leaflet}, NO \code{sf}, NO \code{ggplot2} and NO tile server) so it
also works in an offline environment.
}

\subsection{BaseMap source}{

A static map carries no tile imagery, so \code{snMapSRC} selects the colour palette that emulates
the requested ESRI provider: \code{1} renders the "ESRI Ocean BaseMap" look (i.e. a dark
bathymetric-blue sea with pale-blue EEZ shading), and any other value (i.e. the default \code{2})
renders the "ESRI NatGeo World Map" look (i.e. a pale sea-and-land pastel with dark outlines).
The selected provider name is still written to the console via \code{\link[=info.post.note]{info.post.note()}}, so
the run-time narrative matches the dynamic function exactly.
}

\subsection{Spatial layers}{

The SEAFO spatial layers are read at run-time (i.e. with \code{base::load()}) from the package's
\verb{./data/} folder, which is resolved with \code{here::here()}. The function therefore expects to be
run from the R Project (i.e. package source) root - i.e. the directory that holds the \verb{data/}
folder. The layers, in the order they are drawn (i.e. back-to-front, so that the smallest
features end up on top), are:
\itemize{
\item \strong{EEZs} - the SEAFO Exclusive Economic Zones (\code{dfSEAFO_EEZv11.rda}), drawn when
\code{sbShowEEZs = TRUE};
\item \strong{SEAFO CA} - the SEAFO Convention Area (\code{dfSEAFO_CA.rda}), drawn when
\code{sbShowCA = TRUE};
\item \strong{SEAFO Labels} - the eight SEAFO area tags (\code{A}, \code{A1}, \code{B}, \code{B1}, \code{C}, \code{C1}, \code{D} and
\code{D1}), drawn when both \code{sbShowCA = TRUE} and \code{sbShowLabels = TRUE};
\item \strong{SEAFO Closed Areas} - the SEAFO VME Closures (\code{dfSEAFO_ClosedAreas.rda}), drawn when
both \code{sbShowCA = TRUE} and \code{sbShowClosedAreas = TRUE}.
}

Multi-part polygons (i.e. the \code{piece} column) and polygon holes (i.e. the \code{hole} column) are
both honoured, because the rings are painted with \code{graphics::polypath()} under the
even-odd fill rule rather than with one \code{graphics::polygon()} call per ring.
}

\subsection{Map furniture}{

The figure is completed with a 10-degree graticule, labelled lon/lat axes, a frame, a 500 km
scale bar (bottom-left, sized for the plot's own latitude) and - when \code{sbHideWCL = FALSE} - a
legend (top-left) captioned with \code{ssHeaderWCL}. The plotted extent is identical to the
dynamic version's \code{fitBounds()} call (i.e. longitudes \code{-25.25} to \code{31.25} and latitudes
\code{-52.05} to \code{2.75}), and the axes are aspect-ratio corrected for the mean latitude of that
extent so that the map is not stretched east-west.
}

\subsection{Graphics state}{

Any \code{graphics::par()} settings touched by this function are restored on exit, so the function
is safe to call inside an existing multi-panel layout. It does NOT open or close a device -
see \verb{@returns} above for the recommended \code{grDevices::png()} / \code{grDevices::pdf()} pattern.
}

\subsection{Self-identification}{

The function also brackets its own execution with "Function Self-ID" console notifications
(i.e. via \code{\link[=info.post.func.self.id]{info.post.func.self.id()}}), reported against its START and STOP Code Editor
Line Numbers (CELN). These notifications are only printed when \code{sbRunSelfID = TRUE} is passed
through the \code{...} argument.
}
